.SH EXAMPLES
Compute and print 50 factorial, then a square-root approximation:
.PP
.EX
#include <stdio.h>
#include "bignums.h"

int main(void)
{
    BigInt factorial;
    BigFloat two, root;

    if (bigIntFactorial(&factorial, 50) != 0)
        return 1;
    printBigInt(&factorial);
    putchar('\en');

    bigFloatFromUint32(&two, 2);
    if (bigFloatSqrt(&root, &two, 4) != 0)
        return 1;
    printBigFloat(&root, 6);
    putchar('\en');
    return 0;
}
.EE
.SH PRECISION
Float arithmetic rounds toward zero.  Addition, subtraction, and
multiplication retain up to 8192 significant binary bits.
Reciprocal, division, and square root round to 32 * target_limbs bits,
with target_limbs clamped to 1 through 256.
Exponent range errors are reported instead of overflowing signed arithmetic.
There are no NaN or infinity representations, configurable rounding modes,
or decimal float input routine.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Most arithmetic routines return 0 on success, with INT_MAX or \-1 indicating
failure.  Comparisons, bit lookup, and scalar division and modulo return
values instead; consult the individual function pages.
Integer scalar arithmetic and parsing may leave partial results on failure.
Float arithmetic leaves outputs unchanged on error.
The void float truncation and printing routines report range errors by
setting errno to ERANGE.
.SH BUGS
The library remains experimental and does not validate number pointers,
size fields, or signs.  Integer FFT multiplication uses double-precision
transforms without independent verification of the recovered product.
The regression suite includes small-value numerical oracles and selected
capacity and exponent boundary checks; it does not establish correctness
across the full precision and exponent range.
The float printer supports at most 2560 decimal places and an 8192-bit
integer part.  Unsupported output sizes set errno to ERANGE without output.
Read the function pages before using boundary-sized inputs or precision.
.SH HISTORY
The project began as a side quest for Tupper's self-referential formula and
other mathematical experiments.
